\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Chapter 8: System Estimation by Instrumental Variables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
SIV and GMM
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.2 General Linear System of Equations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now discuss estimation of a linear panel data model of the form
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{it}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+u_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Note, there is a common vector, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, in each time period.
 Nevertheless, this notation allows for different intercepts and slopes
 because 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 can contain time period dummies and interactions between time dummies and
 covariates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Stacking over 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
y_{i} & = & x_{i}\beta+u_{i}\\
x_{i} & = & \left(\begin{array}{c}
x_{i1}^{\prime}\\
\vdots\\
x_{iT}^{\prime}
\end{array}\right)\nonumber 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 vector, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times K$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 vector errors.
 Note, the rows of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 can represent different time periods for the same cross-sectional unit.
 Therefore, the following analysis applies to panel data models where 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is small relative to the cross section sample size, 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{it}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{t}\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of instruments for each time period, in the sense that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(z_{it}u_{it})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

then we can setup the matrix of instruments as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{i}=\left(\begin{array}{ccc}
z_{i1}^{\prime} &  & 0\\
 & \ddots\\
0 &  & z_{iT}^{\prime}
\end{array}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which has dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times L$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $L=L_{1}+L_{2}+\cdots+L_{T}$
\end_inset

.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the same for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(L_{t}=L,\ t=1,\ldots,T)$
\end_inset

, a different setup is possible:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{i}=\left(\begin{array}{c}
z_{i1}^{\prime}\\
\vdots\\
z_{iT}^{\prime}
\end{array}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which also has dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times L$
\end_inset

.
 As we sill see, generally the choice (5) leads to a different estimator
 than when the IVs are chosen as in (4).
 In the important special case where the same instruments, say 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

, can be used for every equation, we can write (5) as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
z_{i}=\left(\begin{array}{c}
w_{i}^{\prime}\\
\vdots\\
w_{i}^{\prime}
\end{array}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The following orthogonality condition is the basis for estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\emph on
SIV.1:
\series default
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i})=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(The abbreviation SIV stands for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

system instrumental variables.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

).
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 has structure (4), then for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}=\left(\begin{array}{c}
z_{i1}u_{i1}\\
\vdots\\
z_{iT}u_{iT}
\end{array}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and so 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 reproduces the orthogonality conditions (3).
 We also assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 On the other hand, the choice of instrument matrix in (5) means we are
 only using the moment conditions aggregated across time, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{t=1}^{T}E(z_{it}u_{it})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, to obtain the efficient GMM estimator that uses only the moments
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{it}^{\prime}u_{it})=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

, the matrix of instruments should be as in (4) because this expresses the
 full set of moment conditions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An assumption sufficient for identification is the 
\series bold
rank condition:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\emph on
SIV.2:
\series default
\emph default
 
\emph on
rank 
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i})=K$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i})$
\end_inset

 is an 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times K$
\end_inset

 matrix, Assumption SIV.2 requires the columns of this matrix to be linearly
 independent.
 Necessary for the rank conditions is the 
\series bold
order condition:
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $L\geq K$
\end_inset

.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 has structure (4), then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i})=\left(\begin{array}{c}
E(z_{i1}x_{i1}^{\prime})\\
\vdots\\
E(z_{iT}x_{iT}^{\prime})
\end{array}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which has dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times K$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{it}x_{it}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 has dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{t}\times K$
\end_inset

.
 Assumption SIV.2 requires that this matrix have full column rank.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.3 Generalized Method of Moments Estimation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.3.1 General Weighting Matrix
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The orthogonality conditions in Assumption SIV.1 suggest an estimation strategy.
 Under Assumptions SIV.1 and SIV.2, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is the 
\emph on
unique
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 vector solving the linear set population moment conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E[z_{i}^{\prime}(y_{i}-x_{i}\beta)]=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

(That 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is a solution follows from Assumption SIV.1; that it is unique follows by
 Assumption SIV.2.) This equation shows that 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is identified.
 Because sample averages are consistent estimators of population moments,
 the analogy principle applied to condition (8) suggest choosing the estimator
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 to solve
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}^{\prime}(y_{i}-x_{i}\hat{\beta})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Equation (9) is a set of 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 linear equations in the 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 unknowns in 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

.
 First, consider the case 
\begin_inset Formula $L=K$
\end_inset

, so that we have exactly enough IVs for the explanatory variables in the
 system.
 Then, if the 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times K$
\end_inset

 matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is nonsingular, we can solve for 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}_{IV}=\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}^{\prime}y_{i}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We can write 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 using full matrix notation as 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}=(Z^{\prime}X)^{-1}Z^{\prime}Y$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $NT\times L$
\end_inset

 matrix obtained by stacking 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,N$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $NT\times K$
\end_inset

 matrix obtained by stacking 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,N$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $NT\times1$
\end_inset

 vector obtained from stacking 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,N$
\end_inset

.
 We call (10) the 
\series bold
system IV (SIV)
\series default
 
\series bold
estimator
\series default
.
 Application of the law of large numbers shows that the SIV estimator is
 consistent under Assumptions SIV.1 and SIV.2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When 
\begin_inset Formula $L>K$
\end_inset

, so that we have more columns in the IV matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 than we need for identification, choosing 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is more complicated.
 Except in special cases, (10) will not have a solution.
 Instead, we choose 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 to make the vector in equation (10) as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

small
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 as possible in the sample.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To obtain a general class of estimators, we obtain a 
\series bold
weighting matrix
\series default
, and then we minimize the squared weighted Euclidean length of the 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times1$
\end_inset

 vector in equation (10).
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}$
\end_inset

 be an 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times L$
\end_inset

 symmetric, positive semidefinite matrix, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}$
\end_inset

 is generally an estimator.
 A 
\series bold
generalized method of moments (GMM) estimator
\series default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is a vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 that solves the problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \min_{b}}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}^{\prime}(y_{i}-x_{i}b)\right]^{\prime}\hat{W}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}^{\prime}(y_{i}-x_{i}b)\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Because expression (11) is a quadratic function of 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

, the solution to it has a closed form.
 Using multivariable calculus or direct substitution, we can show that the
 unique solution is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}=\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)\hat{W}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)\right]^{-1}\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}}\right)\hat{W}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{i}^{\prime}y_{i}\right)\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

assuming that 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)\hat{W}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)\right]$
\end_inset

 is nonsingular.
 We can also write 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 using full matrix notation as 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}=(X^{\prime}Z\hat{W}Z^{\prime}X)^{-1}(X^{\prime}Z\hat{W}Z^{\prime}Y)$
\end_inset

.
 To show that this estimator is consistent, we assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}$
\end_inset

 has a nonsingular probability limit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\emph on
SIV.3:
\emph default
 
\series default

\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}W$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 is a nonrandom, symmetric, 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times L$
\end_inset

 positive definite matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In applications, the convergence in Assumption SIV.3 will follow from the
 law of large numbers because 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}$
\end_inset

 will be a function of sample averages.
 The fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 is assume to be positive definite means that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}$
\end_inset

 is positive definite with probability approaching one.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
8.1 (Consistency of GMM):
\series default
 
\emph on
Under Assumptions SIV.1-SIV.3, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}\beta$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}=\beta+\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)\hat{W}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)\right]^{-1}\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}}\right)\hat{W}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}\right)\right]
\]

\end_inset

Under Assumption SIV.2, 
\begin_inset Formula $C\equiv E(z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i})$
\end_inset

 has rank 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

, and combining with Assumption SIV.3, 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\prime}WC$
\end_inset

 has rank 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 and is therefore nonsingular.
 It follows by the law of large numbers that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{p\lim}\ \hat{\beta}=\beta+(C^{\prime}WC)^{-1}C^{\prime}W(\mbox{p\lim}\ N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i})=\beta+(C^{\prime}WC)^{-1}C^{\prime}W\cdot0=\beta$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Theorem 8.1 shows that a large class of estimators is consistent for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 under Assumptions SIV.1 and SIV.2, provided taht we choose 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}$
\end_inset

 to satisfy modest restrictions.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $L=K$
\end_inset

, the GMM estimator (12) becomes equation (10), no matter how we choose
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}$
\end_inset

 because 
\begin_inset Formula $X^{\prime}Z$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times K$
\end_inset

 nonsingular matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can also show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is asymptotically normally distributed under these first three assumptions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
8.2 (Asymptotic Normality of GMM):
\series default
 
\emph on
Under Assumptions SIV.1-SIV.3, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}(\hat{\beta}-\beta)$
\end_inset

 is asymptotically normally distributed withmean zero and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{\emph{AVar}}\sqrt{N}(\hat{\beta}-\beta)=(C^{\prime}WC)^{-1}C^{\prime}W\Lambda WC(C^{\prime}WC)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Lambda\equiv E(z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}z_{i})=\mbox{\emph{Var}}(z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The asymptotic variance matrix in equation (13) looks complicated, but it
 can be consistently estimated.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Lambda}$
\end_inset

 is a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda$
\end_inset

, then equation (13) is consistently estimated by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[\left(\nicefrac{X^{\prime}Z}{N}\right)\hat{W}\left(\nicefrac{Z^{\prime}X}{N}\right)\right]^{-1}\left(\nicefrac{X^{\prime}Z}{N}\right)\hat{W}\hat{\Lambda}\hat{W}\left(\nicefrac{Z^{\prime}X}{N}\right)\left[\left(\nicefrac{X^{\prime}Z}{N}\right)\hat{W}\left(\nicefrac{Z^{\prime}X}{N}\right)\right]^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

As usual, we estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta})$
\end_inset

 by dividing expression (15) by 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

While the general formula is occaionally useful, it turns out that it is
 greatly simplified by choosing 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}$
\end_inset

 appropriately.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.3.2 System Two-Stage Least Squares Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}$
\end_inset

 that leads to a useful and familiar-looking estimator is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{W}=\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)^{-1}=\left(\nicefrac{Z^{\prime}Z}{N}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[E(z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i})\right]^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
 Assumption SIV.3 simply requires that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i})$
\end_inset

 exist and be nonsingular, and these requirements are not very restrictive.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When we plug equation (16) into equation (12) and cancel 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 everywhere, we get
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}=\left[X^{\prime}Z(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime}X\right]^{-1}X^{\prime}Z(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime}Y
\]

\end_inset

This looks just like the single-equation 2SLS estimator, and so we call
 it the 
\series bold
system 2SLS (S2SLS) estimator
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For the panel data model, when the instrument matrix is stacked as in (5),
 which means that we have the same number of IVs for each time period, the
 S2SLS estimator reduces to the 
\series bold
pooled 2SLS (P2SLS) estimator
\series default
.
 In other words, one treats the stacked panel data as a long cross section
 and performs standard 2SLS.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 takes the form in (4), the S2SLS estimator has a different characterization.
 Namely, we run a first-stage regression separately for each 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{it}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,N$
\end_inset

, and obtain the fitted values, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{it}^{\prime}=z_{it}^{\prime}\hat{\Pi}_{t}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Pi}_{t}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{t}\times K$
\end_inset

.
 Then we obtain the pooled IV estimator using 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{it}$
\end_inset

 (which is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

) as the IVs for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 The key difference in the two approaches is that the P2SLS estimator pools
 across time in estimating the reduced form, while the second procedure
 estimates a separate reduced form for each 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the next subsection, we will see that the S2SLS estimator is not necessarily
 the asymptotically efficeint estimator.
 Still, it is 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}$
\end_inset

-consistent and easy to compute given the data matrices 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.3.3 Optimal Weighting Matrix
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Given that a GMM estimator exists for any positive definite weighting matrix,
 it is important to have a way of choosing among all of the possibilities.
 It turns out that there is a choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 that produces the GMM estimator with the smallest asymptotic variance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The expression (13) simplifies to 
\begin_inset Formula $(C^{\prime}\Lambda C)^{-1}$
\end_inset

 if we set 
\begin_inset Formula $W\equiv\Lambda^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
 Using standard arguments from matrix algebra, it can be shown that 
\begin_inset Formula $(C^{\prime}WC)^{-1}C^{\prime}W\Lambda WC(C^{\prime}WC)^{-1}-(C^{\prime}\Lambda C)^{-1}$
\end_inset

 is positive semidefinite for any 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times L$
\end_inset

 positive definite matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

.
 The easiest way to prove this point is to show that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(C^{\prime}\Lambda C)-(C^{\prime}WC)(C^{\prime}W\Lambda WC)^{-1}(C^{\prime}WC)
\]

\end_inset

is positive semidefinite.
 This discussion motivates the following assumption and theorem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\shape up
SIV.4:
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $W=\Lambda^{-1}$
\end_inset

, 
\emph on
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda$
\end_inset

 is defined by express (14)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\emph on
8.3 (Optimal Weighting Matrix).

\series default
 
\emph default
Under Assumptions SIV.1-SIV.4, the resulting GMM estimator is efficient among
 all GMM estimators of the form (12).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Provided that we can consistently estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda$
\end_inset

, we can obtain the asymptotically efficient GMM estimator.
 Any consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda$
\end_inset

 delivers the efficient GMM estimator, but one estimator is commonly used
 that imposes no structure on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Algorithm*

\series bold
\shape up
8.1 (GMM with Optimal Weighting Matrix):
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{\beta}$
\end_inset

 be an initial consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 In most case, this is the S2SLS estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\shape italic
\emph on
Obtain the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 residual vectors
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\check{u}_{i}=y_{i}-x_{i}\check{\beta},\ i=1,\ldots,N
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\shape italic
\emph on
A generally consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Lambda}=N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}^{\prime}\check{u}_{i}\check{u}_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\shape italic
\emph on
Choose
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{W}\equiv\hat{\Lambda}^{-1}=\left(N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}Z_{i}^{\prime}\check{u}_{i}\check{u}_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda$
\end_inset

 in part c of Algorithm 8.1 is consistent for 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}z_{i})$
\end_inset

 under general conditions.
 When each row of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 represent different time periods, so we have a single-equation panel data
 model, the estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Lambda}$
\end_inset

 allows for arbitrary heteroskedasticity (conditional or unconditional),
 as well as arbitrary serial correlation (conditional or unconditional).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The asymptotic variance of the optimal GMM estimator is estimated as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[\left(X^{\prime}Z\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}\hat{u}_{i}\hat{u}_{i}z_{i}\right)^{-1}\left(Z^{\prime}X\right)\right]^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{i}\equiv y_{i}-X_{i}\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

.
 The square roots of diagonal elements of this matrix are the asymptotic
 standard errors of the optimal GMM estimator.
 This estimator is called a 
\series bold
minimum chi-square estimator
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}=x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and the 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

 are the system OLS residuals, expression (19) becomes the robust variances
 matrix estimator for SOLS.
 This expression reduces to the robust variances matrix esimator for FGLS
 when 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}=\hat{\Omega}^{-1}x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and the 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

 are the FGLS residuals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.3.4 The Generalized Method of Moments Three-Stage Least Squares Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The GMM estimator using weighing matrix (18) places no restrictions on either
 the unconditional or conditional (on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

) variance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Nevertheless, it is still common to assume that the conditional variance
 matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 is constant.
 This assumption leads to the 
\series bold
GMM three-stage least squares (GMM 3SLS) estimator
\series default
.
 To define the 3SLS estimator, let 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{u}_{i}=y_{i}-x_{i}\check{\beta}$
\end_inset

 be the residuals from an initial estimation, usually S2SLS.
 Define the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times T$
\end_inset

 matrix
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\Omega}\equiv N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\check{u}_{i}\check{u}_{i}^{\prime}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Note, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Omega}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}\Omega=E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 The weighting matrix used by 3SLS is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{W}=\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}z_{i}\right)^{-1}=\left[\nicefrac{Z^{\prime}\left(I_{N}\otimes\hat{\Omega}\right)Z}{N}\right]^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This equation always consistently estimates 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[E(z_{i}^{\prime}\Omega z_{I})\right]^{-1}$
\end_inset

, and plugging it into (12) gives the 3SLS estimator
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}=\left[X^{\prime}Z\left\{ Z^{\prime}\left(I_{N}\otimes\hat{\Omega}\right)Z\right\} ^{-1}Z^{\prime}X\right]^{-1}X^{\prime}Z\left\{ Z^{\prime}\left(I_{N}\otimes\hat{\Omega}\right)Z\right\} ^{-1}Z^{\prime}Y
\end{equation}

\end_inset

By Theorems 8.1 and 8.2, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is consistent and asymptotically normal under Assumptions SIV.1-SIV.3.
 Assumption SIV.3 requires 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}^{\prime}\Omega z_{i})$
\end_inset

 to be nonsingular, a standard assumption.
 Important to note, the computation of the 3SLS estimator does not require
 three stages.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When is 3SLS an efficient GMM estimator? From Theorem 8.3, equation (21)
 is an efficient weighting matrix provided 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}^{\prime}\Omega z_{i})=\Lambda=E(z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}z_{i})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\shape up
SIV.5:
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}z_{i})=E(z_{i}^{\prime}\Omega z_{i})$
\end_inset

, 
\emph on
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega\equiv E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assumption SIV.5 is the system extension of the homoskedastiticy assumption
 for 2SLS estimation of a single equation.
 A sufficient condition for Assumption SIV.5, one that is easier to interpret,
 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|z_{i})=E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We do not take (23) as the homoskedasticity assumption because there are
 interesting applications where Assumption SIV.5 holds but equation (23)
 does not.
 When
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{i}|z_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is assumed in place of Assumption SIV.1, then equation (23) is equivalent
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(u_{i}|z_{i})=\mbox{Var}(u_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 It is important to see that the elements of the unconditional variance
 matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 are 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 resticted: 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{g}^{2}=\mbox{Var}(u_{g})$
\end_inset

 can change across 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{gh}=\mbox{Cov}(u_{g},u_{h})$
\end_inset

 can differ across 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assumption SIV.5 implies that Assumption SIV.4 holds.
 Therefore, we have the following theorem:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
8.4 (Optimality of 3SLS):
\series default
 
\emph on
Under Assumptions SIV.1, SIV.2, SIV.3, and SIV.5, the 3SLS estimator is an optimal
 GMM estimator.
 Futher the appropriate estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{A}Var}(\hat{\beta})$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[(X^{\prime}Z)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}z_{i}\right)^{-1}(Z^{\prime}X)\right]^{-1}=\left[X^{\prime}Z\left\{ Z^{\prime}\left(I_{N}\otimes\hat{\Omega}\right)Z\right\} Z^{\prime}X\right]^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is important to understand the implications of this theorem.
 First, without Assumption SIV.5, the 3SLS estimator is generally less efficient,
 asymptotically, than the minimum chi-square estimator, and the asymptotic
 variance estimator for 3SLS (25) is inappropriate.
 Second, even with Assumption SIV.5, the 3SLS estimator is no more asymptotically
 efficient than the minimm chi-square estimator: expressions (21) and (25)
 are both consistent estimators of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda^{-1}$
\end_inset

 under Assumption SIV.5.
 In other words, the estimators based on these two different choices for
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}$
\end_inset

 are 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}$
\end_inset

-equivalent under Assumption SIV.5 .
\end_layout

